The '''Tistaina Canal''' (originally known as '''Tistaina Creek''', from [[Kurupiran languages|Kuarupíba]] ''tistîga'' {{Literal translation|barren}}) is a {{convert|9.40|km|mi|-long|adj=mid}} [[W:Canal|canal]] between the [[Boroughs of Castejón|boroughs]] of [[Ampurias]] and [[Expansión]] in the [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]], on the westernmost portion of the [[Akãjú|Akãjú peninsula]]. The canal was created in the mid-18th century from local tidal [[W:Wetland|wetlands]] and freshwater streams, it became a vital conduit for cargo transportation. It saw decreasing use as domestic [[W:Maritime transport|shipping]] declined, and was redeveloped following the closure of the [[Port of Sástago]] from 1993. 

Today it is used for [[W:Water transportation|water transport]] ([[W:Water taxi|water taxis]]) and the daily navigation of small boats, tugs, and barges.

Connecting the [[Alagón (river)|Alagón river]] and [[Auquinco Bay|Upper Salto Bay]], the Tistaina Canal borders the neighborhoods of [[Pejú]], [[Santiago, Expansión|Santiago]], and [[Torreón]], all within [[Expansión]], to the west; and [[Commune of Castejón|Ciudad Vieja]] to the east. Seven [[Bridges and tunnels in Castejón#Ampurias|bridges]] or viaducts cross the canal, carrying, from north to south, Confederation Street, [[Calle Sol Bridge|Calle Sol]], Seventh Avenue, the [[Castejón Metropolitan Rapid Transit|Castejón Metro]]'s [[Castejón Metro Iznájar Line|Iznájar viaduct]], Ninth Street, Concordia Avenue, and the [[Tistaina Avenue]].

